"4  \nTIMOTHY C. BURNESS AND MARCO FUSARI\n\nThere is (https://seis.bristol.ac.uk/~tb13602/docs/bf_derangements_final.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; BURNESS AND MARCO FUSARI ... One of the main open problems in this area is a conjecture from the 1980s attributed to John Thompson, which asserts that every finite simple group \\(G\\) has a conjugacy class \\(C\\) such that \\(G = C^2\\). ... (i) If \\(G\\) is an alternating group, a sporadic group, or a rank one group of Lie type, then there exist conjugacy classes \\(C, D\\) of derangements such that\n4  \nTIMOTHY C. BURNESS AND MARCO FUSARI\n\nThere is an extensive literature on so-called *width problems* for finite groups, and for finite simple groups and normal subsets in particular (we refer the reader to Liebeck’s excellent survey article [59]). One of the main open problems in this area is a conjecture from the 1980s attributed to John Thompson, which asserts that every finite simple group \\(G\\) has a conjugacy class \\(C\\) such that \\(G = C^2\\). This has turned out to be a very difficult problem, but there has been significant progress in recent years. For instance, through the work of several authors, the problem has been reduced to groups of Lie type defined over fields with at most 8 elements. It also worth noting that Thompson’s conjecture immediately implies a famous conjecture of Ore from 1951, which asserts that every element in a finite simple group is a commutator. The proof of the latter was completed by Liebeck et al. in [60] using character-theoretic methods.\n\nIt is therefore natural to study the derangement width of finite simple groups and the main result here is the following theorem from [54].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Laplacian spread of digraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.15410)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Then, we prove sharp upper bounds on the Laplacian spread for all polygonal and balanced digraphs. In particular, we show that the validity of the Laplacian spread bound for balanced digraphs is equivalent to the Laplacian spread conjecture for simple undirected graphs, which was conjectured in 2011 and proven in 2021.\nTitle: On the Laplacian spread of digraphs\nAuthors: Wayne Barrett, Thomas R. Cameron, Emily Evans, H. Tracy Hall, Mark Kempton\nDate: Thu Jun 30 16:56:34 2022\n\nIn this article, we extend the notion of the Laplacian spread to simple directed graphs (digraphs) using the restricted numerical range. First, we provide Laplacian spread values for several families of digraphs. Then, we prove sharp upper bounds on the Laplacian spread for all polygonal and balanced digraphs. In particular, we show that the validity of the Laplacian spread bound for balanced digraphs is equivalent to the Laplacian spread conjecture for simple undirected graphs, which was conjectured in 2011 and proven in 2021. Moreover, we prove an equivalent statement for weighted balanced digraphs with weights between $0$ and $1$. Finally, we state several open conjectures that are motivated by empirical data.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCorrigendum to the equivalent statement of the Laplacian Spread Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.02440)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Title: Corrigendum to the equivalent statement of the Laplacian Spread Conjecture ... For a graph $G,$ let $\\alpha(G)$ denote its second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue. ... $ In this paper, we have corrected two conclusions: First, the necessary and sufficient condition for $\\alpha(G) + \\alpha(\\overline{G}) \\geq 1$ is $\\parallel \\bigtriangledown_{x} - \\bigtriangledown_{y} \\parallel^{2} \\geq 1$ rather than $\\parallel \\bigtriangledown_{x} - \\bigtriangledown_{y} \\parallel^{2} \\geq 2$ which has been proved in \\cite{BS} as demonstrated in our study. Second, we show that the Laplacian spread of balanced digraph $\\Gamma$ satisfies $LS(\\Gamma) \\leq n - \\frac{1}{2}$ but not $LS(\\Gamma) \\leq n - 1$ in \\cite{BCEHK}, since inequality $\\parallel \\bigtriangledown_{x} - \\bigtriangledown_{y} \\parallel^{2} \\geq 2$ does not hold.\nTitle: Corrigendum to the equivalent statement of the Laplacian Spread Conjecture\nAuthors: Borchen Li\nDate: Sat Nov  2 10:52:20 2024\n\nFor a graph $G,$ let $\\alpha(G)$ denote its second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue. The Laplacian Spread Conjecture states that $\\alpha(G)+\\alpha(\\overline{G}) \\geq 1,$ where $\\overline{G}$ is the complement of $G.$ In this paper, we have corrected two conclusions: First, the necessary and sufficient condition for $\\alpha(G) + \\alpha(\\overline{G}) \\geq 1$ is $\\parallel \\bigtriangledown_{x} - \\bigtriangledown_{y} \\parallel^{2} \\geq 1$ rather than $\\parallel \\bigtriangledown_{x} - \\bigtriangledown_{y} \\parallel^{2} \\geq 2$ which has been proved in \\cite{BS} as demonstrated in our study. Second, we show that the Laplacian spread of balanced digraph $\\Gamma$ satisfies $LS(\\Gamma) \\leq n - \\frac{1}{2}$ but not $LS(\\Gamma) \\leq n - 1$ in \\cite{BCEHK}, since inequality $\\parallel \\bigtriangledown_{x} - \\bigtriangledown_{y} \\parallel^{2} \\geq 2$ does not hold.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - trcameron/LaplacianSpreadDigraphs: Supplemental code for paper \"On the Laplacian Spread of Digraphs\" · GitHub (https://github.com/trcameron/LaplacianSpreadDigraphs)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;     * Email: trc5475@psu.edu ... The d6files directory includes the d6 format for all polygonal and balanced digraphs up to order 8. ... The pdffiles directory includes all figures from the paper \"On the Laplacian Spread of Digraphs\", which is created from the files in the TeX directory.\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nC++  | C++  |    |  \nPython  | Python  |    |  \nTeX  | TeX  |    |  \ncsvfiles  | csvfiles  |    |  \nd6files  | d6files  |    |  \npdffiles  | pdffiles  |    |  \n.gitignore  | .gitignore  |    |  \nMakefile  | Makefile  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \nmake.inc  | make.inc  |    |  \n\n\n# Laplacian Spread for Digraphs\n\nPython and C++ files for the study of the Laplacian Spread for Digraphs\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Wayne Barrett\n    * Mathematics Department, Brigham Young University, Provo, UT\n    * Email: wb@mathematics.byu.edu\n  * Thomas R. Cameron\n    * Mathematics Department, Penn State Behrend, Erie, PA\n    * Email: trc5475@psu.edu\n  * Emily Evans\n    * Mathematics Department, Brigham Young University, Provo, UT\n    * Email: ejevans@math.byu.edu\n  * [H. Tracy Hall]\n    * Hall Labs LLC, Provo, UT\n    * Email: H.Tracy@gmail.com\n  * Mark Kempton\n    * Mathematics Department, Brigham Young University, Provo, UT\n    * Email: mkempton@mathematics.byu.edu\n\n### Instructions\n\nThe d6files directory includes the d6 format for all polygonal and balanced digraphs up to order 8. The C++ source for the balanced digraphs is included in the C++ directory, while the C++ source code for the polygonal digraphs is located at (https://github.com/trcameron/polygonal-digraphs).\n\nThe csvfiles directory includes all distinct (alpha,alpha_comp) values along with the digraphs that attain those values.\n\nThe pdffiles directory includes all figures from the paper \"On the Laplacian Spread of Digraphs\", which is created from the files in the TeX directory.\n\nThe Python directory includes scripts for viewing all possible (alpha,alpha_comp) values as well as the digraphs that attains those values. See the Makefile commands display_values and display_digraphs to run these scripts.\n\n## About\n\nSupplemental code for paper \"On the Laplacian Spread of Digraphs\"\n\n\nReadme\n\nActivity\n\n### Stars\n\n0 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n1 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA linear algebraic proof of the Laplacian spread conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03711)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Date: Sat Jul  4 05:32:53 2026\n\nFor a graph $G,$ let $α(G)$ denote its second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue. ... $ In this article, we provide a new proof of the Laplacian spread conjecture by means of linear algebra, which is more concise.\nTitle: A linear algebraic proof of the Laplacian spread conjecture\nAuthors: Bocheng Li, Qingzhong Ji\nDate: Sat Jul  4 05:32:53 2026\n\nFor a graph $G,$ let $α(G)$ denote its second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue. The Laplacian Spread Conjecture is that $α(G)+α(\\overline{G}) \\geq 1,$ where $\\overline{G}$ is the complement of $G.$ In this article, we provide a new proof of the Laplacian spread conjecture by means of linear algebra, which is more concise.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Laplacian spread of digraphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2206.15410v1)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; DOI: 10.1016/j.laa.2023.01.016 ... Then, we prove sharp upper bounds on the Laplacian spread for all polygonal and balanced digraphs. In particular, we show that the validity of the Laplacian spread bound for balanced digraphs is equivalent to the Laplacian spread conjecture for simple undirected graphs, which was conjectured in 2011 and proven in 2021.\n\n## On the Laplacian spread of digraphs\n\nWayne Barrett, Thomas R. Cameron, Emily Evans, H. Tracy Hall, Mark Kempton\n\nPublished: 2022-06-30\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.laa.2023.01.016\n\ngraph\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn this article, we extend the notion of the Laplacian spread to simple directed graphs (digraphs) using the restricted numerical range. First, we provide Laplacian spread values for several families of digraphs. Then, we prove sharp upper bounds on the Laplacian spread for all polygonal and balanced digraphs. In particular, we show that the validity of the Laplacian spread bound for balanced digraphs is equivalent to the Laplacian spread conjecture for simple undirected graphs, which was conjectured in 2011 and proven in 2021. Moreover, we prove an equivalent statement for weighted balanced digraphs with weights between $0$ and $1$. Finally, we state several open conjectures that are motivated by empirical data.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimum distance signless Laplacian spread among bipartite graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/333521/minimum-distance-signless-laplacian-spread-among-bipartite-g)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The claim is motivated by computations and comparisons of small examples in the paper; its general validity is left as a problem for further research. ... The conjecture remains unproved publicly, but an unverified submission dated August 26, 2026 argues that the proposed graph is indeed the unique minimizer.\n\nThe 2016 paper formulates the claim that the balanced complete bipartite graph minimizes the distance signless Laplacian spread among connected bipartite graphs of order $n$n, with equality only for that graph. ...   * The claim was checked computationally for all connected bipartite graphs on $4$4 and $5$5 vertices, with further computations supporting it.\n\nbipartite-graphsgraph-invariantsspectral-graph-theory\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 month ago\n\nClaimed progress\n\nThe conjecture remains unproved publicly, but an unverified submission dated August 26, 2026 argues that the proposed graph is indeed the unique minimizer.\n\nThe 2016 paper formulates the claim that the balanced complete bipartite graph minimizes the distance signless Laplacian spread among connected bipartite graphs of order $n$n, with equality only for that graph.\n\nKnown results\n\n  * The claim was checked computationally for all connected bipartite graphs on $4$4 and $5$5 vertices, with further computations supporting it.\n  * Among complete bipartite graphs $K_{a,n-a}$K a,n−a​, the balanced graph minimizes the spread, with equality only in the balanced case.\n  * Extending this comparison to all connected bipartite graphs was left open in 2016.\n\nCommunity submission (unverified), August 26, 2026\n\nA submitted proof argues the full inequality using distance-sum lower bounds for the two parts, Rayleigh-quotient estimates for the distance signless Laplacian, and separate treatment of complete and non-complete bipartite graphs. It claims the equality case is uniquely the balanced complete bipartite graph, but no independent verification is recorded.\n\nCurrent status (as of August 2026): the published literature still treats the conjecture as open, while a community submission claims a proof dated August 26, 2026 that remains unverified.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Miyazaki_M in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AMiyazaki_M+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; @misc{2205.01409, author = {Mitsuhiro Miyazaki}, title = {{N}on-{G}orenstein locus and almost {G}orenstein property of the {E}hrhart ring of the stable set polytope of a cycle graph}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2205.01409}, note = {arXiv:2205.01409v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... Moreover, we show that the conjecture of Hibi and Tsuchiya is true. ... @misc{2201.02957, author = {Mitsuhiro Miyazaki}, title = {{G}orenstein on the punctured spectrum and nearly {G}orenstein property of the {E}hrhart ring of the stable set polytope of an h-perfect graph}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2201.02957}, note = {arXiv:2201.02957v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2205.01409, author = {Mitsuhiro Miyazaki}, title = {{N}on-{G}orenstein locus and almost {G}orenstein property of the {E}hrhart ring of the stable set polytope of a cycle graph}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2205.01409}, note = {arXiv:2205.01409v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nLet $R$ be the Ehrhart ring of the stable set polytope of a cycle graph which is not Gorenstein. We describe the non-Gorenstein locus of $\\mathrm{Spec} R$. Further, we show that $R$ is almost Gorenstein. Moreover, we show that the conjecture of Hibi and Tsuchiya is true.\n\n  * Gorenstein on the punctured spectrum and nearly Gorenstein property of the Ehrhart ring of the stable set polytope of an h-perfect graph\n\nMitsuhiro Miyazaki\n\nJan 11 2022 math.AC math.CO arXiv:2201.02957v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2201.02957, author = {Mitsuhiro Miyazaki}, title = {{G}orenstein on the punctured spectrum and nearly {G}orenstein property of the {E}hrhart ring of the stable set polytope of an h-perfect graph}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2201.02957}, note = {arXiv:2201.02957v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn this paper, we give a criterion of the nearly Gorenstein property of the Ehrhart ring of the stable set polytope of an h-perfect graph: the Ehrhart ring of the stable set polytope of an h-perfect graph $G$ with connected components $G^{(1)}, \\ldots, G^{(\\ell)}$ is nearly Gorenstein if and only if (1) for each $i$, the Ehrhart ring of the stable set polytope of $G^{(i)}$ is Gorenstein and (2) $|\\omega(G^{(i)})-\\omega(G^{(j)})|\\leq 1$ for any $i$ and $j$, where $\\omega(G^{(i)})$ is the clique number of $G^{(i)}$. We also show that the Segre product of Cohen-Macaulay graded rings with linear non-zerodivisor which are Gorenstein on the punctured spectrum is also Gorenstein on the punctured spectrum if all but one rings are standard graded.\n\n  * Non-Gorenstein loci of Ehrhart rings of chain and order polytopes\n\nMitsuhiro Miyazaki, Janet Page\n\n-----------------------------------